      ],
      "fit": 0.85
    },
    {
      "id": "p2",
      "text": "A symbol of the regime's hollow promises broke when critical infrastructure failure hit the Euphrates river system. This disaster outcome was fueled by water management failures that triggered widespread inundation of civilian areas. The upstream power broker, Turkey, conceded only a sliver of accountability—a concession extracted under the weight of tragedy. Meanwhile, the people's livelihoods and futures were washed away, a stark flooding damage to the region's civilian population. The head of state's visit was less an inspection and more a tour of the damage he failed to prevent.",
      "source": {
        "agent_id": "The-Outlaw",
        "fragments": [
          "how-1",
          "how-2",
          "how-3",
          "how-4",
          "how-5",
          "result-1",
          "result-5"
        ],
        "channel_role": "toned"
      },
      "warnings": [
        "how_fragments_expanded_to_contiguous_span"
      ],
      "fit": 0.78
    },
    {
      "id": "p3",
      "text": "In the war-ravaged regions of Deir Ezzor and Raqqa, a heavy rainfall event exposed the system's fragility. Reservoir overflow and embankment failure led to a flooding event that damaged schools, homes, and farms—agricultural loss that crippled the already vulnerable. The authorities scrambled to manage the optics, deploying a military branch for a rescue mission only after the pediatric fatalities from flooding became undeniable. This was not a natural disaster alone, but a failure of state leadership and a national disaster outcome.",
      "source": {
